Think of traditional batch ingestion like taking a single photograph of your database every night at midnight. 
Change Data Capture, or CDC, is like setting up a security camera: it records a continuous, real-time video stream of every single INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE exactly as they happen.

Technical Breakdown :- 
Traditional pipelines rely on query-based extraction, executing a SELECT * WHERE updated_at > last_run against the database.
This approach is slow, degrades production performance, and fundamentally misses intermediate changes. True CDC is log-based.
Every operational database (like Postgres or MySQL) maintains a Write-Ahead Log (WAL) or Binlog for its own internal crash recovery.
Modern CDC tools, like Debezium or AWS DMS, silently read this underlying log file. They instantly translate raw database transactions into an event 
stream—typically fed into Apache Kafka—without executing a single SQL query or placing any read-locks on the live tables.

Buisness Context :- 
In modern business, batch data is often dead data. If an e-commerce customer adds an item to their cart, changes the quantity, and then deletes it over the course of an hour,
a nightly batch job will completely miss that behavioral data. CDC enables real-time use cases like dynamic pricing algorithms, live fraud detection, and micro-segmentation, 
giving the business an absolute, second-by-second pulse on user behavior.

Design Decision :- 
Engineers often try to build 'real-time' pipelines by writing aggressive cron jobs that poll the database every 60 seconds,
which rapidly triggers a CPU meltdown on the production server. As a data engineer, I mandate log-based CDC for real-time ingestion.
However, the architectural tradeoff is complex state management: CDC produces a massive append-only log of granular events.
To make this data usable for analysts, I architect a downstream pipeline using Spark Structured Streaming and Apache Iceberg, 
which continuously applies those millions of updates and deletes to maintain a perfectly mirrored, queryable replica in the data lake.
